# Project Versioning

- Product releases use SemVer and are stored as `productVersion` in `gradle.properties`.
- `productVersion=0.0.0` means the product has not been released yet.
- Do not use the product release version as the Gradle version for all modules.
- Each module owns its SemVer version and changelog. Keep them inside the module when the module exists.
- The non-detachable `root` module uses `rootVersion` in `gradle.properties` and `CHANGELOG.md`.
- `RELEASES.md` is only the product release manifest. Update it for product releases, not for every module change.
- The top-level `README.md` is the project/product overview. Update it when cutting a product release (`productVersion`), not with ordinary module or `root` infra commits.
- When changing a module version, verify the matching module changelog entry exists.
- When cutting a product release, verify `RELEASES.md`, `productVersion`, the listed module versions, and the top-level `README.md` Status agree.

## Git Alignment

Version numbers and changelog version sections are updated only during commit
preparation (see `.cursor/rules/agent-git-workflow.mdc`), not during ordinary
implementation.

Before bumping or changing a module or product version, check git history for that
module:

- Use `git log`, `git diff`, and the last committed version/changelog for the
  module as the source of truth for what has actually shipped.
- A version bump must reflect a change that is being committed now, not only
  local uncommitted work that may still be revised or discarded.
- Do not bump a version for exploratory or interim work that is rolled back
  before commit.

When reverting or discarding local work that was never committed:

- Do not add a changelog entry for the reverted change.
- If a changelog entry or version bump was added only for that uncommitted work,
  remove it and restore the last committed version value.
- Do not treat local-only pivots (for example a temporary UI replaced before the
  first module commit) as a released version that deserves its own changelog
  section.

If git history and local version/changelog files disagree, reconcile toward git
unless the user is explicitly preparing a new commit that will update both.

## Commit Checks

When reviewing or preparing a commit that changes any version value:

- Confirm against git what the previous committed version was and what is new in
  this commit.
- Verify the version bump follows SemVer and belongs to the changed module or product release.
- Verify the matching changelog entry contains the main reason for the version change.
- For `productVersion` changes, verify `RELEASES.md` contains the release summary and module versions, and that top-level `README.md` reflects the new product Status.
- For module version changes, verify the changelog lives with that module and the commit does not mix unrelated modules.
- Enforce `.cursor/rules/module-commit-boundaries.mdc`: version, changelog, and implementation changes must stay within one module per commit.
- If version, changelog, and release manifest are inconsistent, flag it before committing.
